Model Answer:
The Board of Discipline (BOD) can impose the following sanctions on a student found guilty of a disciplinary offence:

(a) Issue a letter of warning to the student;  
(b) Stipulate a written undertaking by the student, including conditions and further sanctions if not met;  
(c) Require restitution accepted by the student, complainant, and BOD;  
(d) Stipulate any form of service contributing to the welfare of the student community as a penalty;  
(e) Mandate counselling or medical treatment;  
(f) Deprive the student of a pass in any examination, assignment, quiz, or class test;  
(g) Impose a fine not exceeding $10,000;  
(h) Expel or suspend the student permanently or temporarily, including exclusion from the University, programmes, courses, or examinations;  
(i) Deprive the student of any degree, diploma, certificate, or academic distinction awarded or to be awarded.

Only students expelled, suspended, fined more than $5,000, or deprived of academic awards have the right to appeal to the Appeal Committee [SOURCE 1], [SOURCE 2].
